\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{The Gaussian partition function and probability law}
\date{2026-11-10}

\p{Let #{Q} be a \vocabk{positive precision parameter}{fdip-0004}
on a finite real inner-product space #{(E,h)}, with canonical volume
#{\lambda_E}. Its \newvocab{Gaussian partition function} is
##{Z_Q=\int_E w_Q\,\extd\lambda_E,
\qquad w_Q(x)=\ue^{-h(x,Qx)/2}.}
The normalization theorem \ref{fdip-002T} proves
##{0<Z_Q=\frac{(2\pi)^{n/2}}{\sqrt{\det Q}}<\infty,
\qquad n=\dim E.}
Its \newvocab{Gaussian probability law} is the Borel measure given,
for Borel #{A\subseteq E}, by
##{\mu_Q(A)=Z_Q^{-1}\int_A w_Q\,\extd\lambda_E.}}
\p{The coordinate densities in \ref{fdip-00JS} realize this measure
in counting and orthonormal eigencharts. Its probability-measure properties,
zero-dimensional case and reference-rescaling identity are
\ref{fdip-00JT}. A finite-sum
\vocabk{partition function}{ftip-008J} has the same normalizing role.
A finite vertex set gives a finite-dimensional configuration space.
Integrability of its Gaussian weight follows from the normalization
theorem, using positive precision.}
\p{The density convention is compared with
\citet{Lemma B.54 and Proposition B.55, pp. 504-505}{friedli2017statistical};
that source uses covariance rather than precision.
The normalization failures in \ref{fdip-002U} distinguish infinite normalizers,
zero weights and the zero-dimensional case.}
